    {
      "question": "Explain Fuzhou shadow puppetry",
      "status": 200,
      "elapsed_ms": 2711.481992,
      "answer": {
        "answer_id": "ans_083f86e61a5147b2a376e8482b064815",
        "locale": "en",
        "depth": "beginner",
        "status": "answered",
        "uncertainty": "partial",
        "reason": "supported_explanation",
        "answer_text": "Fuzhou shadow puppetry is listed as traditional theatre from Wafangdian, Liaoning.\n\nShadow puppetry is a traditional form of storytelling where flat, often jointed figures are held behind a lit screen, so the audience sees their shadows. Think of it like an early kind of animation: the puppeteer moves the figures and speaks or sings the characters' parts, while music and sometimes narration accompany the show. The puppets are usually made from leather or similar materials, cut and colored to create detailed silhouettes. Because the performance combines visual art, music, and oral storytelling, it is often described as a form of traditional theatre. A useful question to ask is: how does the shadow itself become part of the story?",
        "claims": [
          {
            "claim_id": "claim_1",
            "text": "Fuzhou shadow puppetry is listed as traditional theatre from Wafangdian, Liaoning.",
